-- title:
--   Nonzero section of a line bundle is nonvanishing at the generic point
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme satisfying `IsIntegral`, and let $M$ be an object of `X.Modules`. Assume `hM : Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible M`, that is, every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ such that the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules on $U$. Let $s$ be a morphism from the monoidal unit $\mathbf{1}_{X.\mathrm{Modules}}$ to $M$, i.e. a global section of $M$, and assume $s \neq 0$. Consider `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal s`, defined as the infimum, in the lattice of ideal sheaf data on $X$, of those $J$ for which the inequality $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}(s, U) \le J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ holds on every affine open $U$ of $X$; here $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}(s, U)$ is the ideal of $\Gamma(X, U)$ generated by the range of `Scheme.Modules.coeff s U`, the coefficients read off from $s$ over $U$ through the available trivialisations of $M$ there. The conclusion is that the generic point of $X$ does not belong to the support of this ideal sheaf datum, so the closed subscheme it cuts out — the zero scheme of $s$ — is a proper closed subset of $X$.
--
--   This is the statement that the divisor of zeros of a nonzero global section of an invertible sheaf on an integral scheme is a proper closed subscheme, in the form needed for the zero-scheme ideal attached to a section. It is used in the study of polarisations and in the results describing the support of `zeroSchemeIdeal` under specialisation and in Krull dimension at most one, and rests on the local computation identifying the ideal of the zero scheme over a trivialising affine open with the ideal generated by the coefficient of $s$ there.
